00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:55.680
but you can even
modify what kind

00:28:55.680 --> 00:28:58.650
of Windows features are enabled.

00:28:58.650 --> 00:29:01.080
So it could be SMTP,
it could be --

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:03.390
you could even install
Windows Services,

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:07.440
or you can even install an
MSMQ client if you have to.

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:11.340
So pretty much you have
the entire flexibility

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:15.990
that you require for your
applications to remove

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:19.500
and improve with Managed
Instance on App Service.

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:26.680
Let me go back to the browser,
select the storage account.

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:35.350
I will now select
the container name.

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:39.420
And I'll give the
name of the zip file.

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:42.490
So name of my zip file is --

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:46.500
and again, the name of the
zip file can be anything,

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:48.900
it's just that the
installation script file has

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:52.210
to be named at installed.ps1.

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:58.780
And then you go ahead
and select the identity.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:05.340
Now, one important
point to note here is

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:08.730
in case your application
doesn't have a dependency,

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:10.920
you can completely
ignore this step.

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:14.040
It's not mandatory that you need
to have an installation script.

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:16.080
If you don't need it,
don't include it,

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:20.430
but this is optional,
yet super powerful.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:21.900
Now, next, click "Deployment".

00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:24.150
Now, what happens
on this tab is --

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:26.910
essentially this is the
same experience that you --

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:29.520
most of us are used to.

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:33.810
What's happening on this
tab is that if your --

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:35.820
we are creating an app
at the end of the day,

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:38.610
and if you have source
codes sitting in GitHub,

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:42.810
you can basically configure that
on this page, and we'll go ahead

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:44.520
and pull the code and deploy it

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.640
on the web app
once it's created.

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:50.280
The next step is
configuring the networking.

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:53.070
So you -- depending
on your need,

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.500
you enable the public access.

00:30:55.500 --> 00:31:01.260
And now, another major
difference here is that instead

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:04.170
of you having to
configure virtual network

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:07.110
for every individual
application,

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:09.120
what we are now going
to do is we are going

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:12.660
to create an app service
plan for Managed Instance,

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:17.520
and then the entire plan
is configured to be part

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:19.260
of the virtual network.

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:23.040
So that effectively means all
the web applications created

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:26.710
within the plan are
part of the VNet.

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:30.640
I'll go ahead and
select the VNet.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:38.130
And then, you need to enable
VNet integration and you need

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:44.520
to select a subnet for
your web applications.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:47.170
I will next click "Review".

00:31:49.950 --> 00:31:52.680
Once it is ready to be created,
I'll click on "Create".

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:56.700
Now, what will happen behind
the scenes is we will go ahead

00:31:56.700 --> 00:32:01.620
and provision an instance
size that you have chosen,

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:06.030
and then we will go ahead and
provision an app service plan.

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:10.530
We will download the zip file,
run on the installation script,

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:14.370
and then if you have given us
the source code repo link,

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:16.380
we will go ahead and pull
the code and deploy.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:18.180
So that's how simple it can be.

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:22.680
So now in the interest
of time, I've gone ahead

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:27.780
and actually created a Managed
Instance on App Service plan,

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:29.640
and this is how it is.

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:33.630
So as you can see, evidently,
this is very, very similar

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:38.730
to what existing app service
plan looks and feels like.

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:40.920
So no major differences other

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:45.390
than you see a new option
called as "Configuration".

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:47.790
So this is pretty much
what I showed you

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:50.310
that I was trying to configure.

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:52.680
So here you have the
storage account,

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:55.110
you have the script
container, you have the name

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:57.630
of the zip file, which
are all your installers

00:32:57.630 --> 00:33:03.390
and your installation script.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:07.710
Now, my application requires
different storage mounts,

00:33:07.710 --> 00:33:14.320
so I have three storage mounts,
map to K:, L: -- sorry, and H:.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.570
Now, if I have to create
a new storage mount,

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:24.210
I can just give it a
name, maybe "Storage 4,"

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:26.820
and this will either be
mapped to an Azure file.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:30.450
If that's the case, you
again select this name

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:32.280
of your storage account.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:39.450
Once you do that -- oh, you'll
now select the file share.

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:43.290
And again, the
entire connectivity

00:33:43.290 --> 00:33:48.630
between your application and
the file share is secured using

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:49.680
a secret.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:52.020
So your connection
string is actually saved

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:53.550
as a key vault secret.

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:58.530
So you need to give
us a key vault name.

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:00.210
Make this the key vault,
and then you go ahead

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:03.390
and select the mapped secret.

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:05.880
And then you'll give
us the drive letter.

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:10.020
So maybe I can call it "G:".

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:11.760
So what this means is --

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:18.270
-- that basically I'm
going to map a G:

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:20.910
as to the Azure file
share, which is created

00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:22.860
with the name "Mount FS".

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:26.760
Now, this could also be a UNC.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:29.250
But as Andrew
mentioned, as long --

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:31.260
this could be a file
server on Azure

00:34:31.260 --> 00:34:35.910
within your own data center,
as long as it is accessible

00:34:35.910 --> 00:34:40.350
to the virtual network that
this application is tagged to;

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:44.070
or it could just be
a local file share.

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:47.760
Remember, local mounts
are the formally nature,

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:52.860
meaning for any reason if
this instance gets restarted,

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:56.820
any content which is on this
local file share will get lost.

00:34:56.820 --> 00:34:58.470
So please do remember that.

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:00.270
And again, when it comes
to local file share,

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:02.370
you just select the drive part.

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:05.640
And that's how simple it
is for you to go ahead

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:07.680
and create new mounts.

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:11.070
Similar for registries,
as I showed in the code

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:12.630
that my application depends

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:15.300
on reading the database
disconnection string

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:17.010
from registry.

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:19.290
If I have to create
a new registry,

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:21.120
you again give us
a registry path,

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:26.640
something like
"HKEY.LOCAL.MACHINE/Software".

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.970
And now you will again
have to specify the name

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:35.130
of the key vault where the
actual registry value is stored

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:37.290
as a key vault secret.

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:41.610
So let me go ahead and
select the key vault.

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:45.060
And as you can see,
this pulls the secrets.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:48.360
So that's my secret, now the
registry could be a string order

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:49.260
D word.

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:51.120
So again, that's how
easy we make it for you

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:53.460
to even create registry
files for you.

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:56.730
And now, "registry", I am
defining a registry as part

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:59.010
of the code, but
imagine a scenario

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:02.880
where you are installing
a component which needs

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:05.580
to write a license file
to a local registry,

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:08.580
so even that's possible now.

00:36:08.580 --> 00:36:10.590
I'll click on "Cancel".

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:14.760
And yes, I think by now, you
all know that Managed Instance

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:19.740
on App Service does support
secured remote connectivity

00:36:19.740 --> 00:36:21.420
using Bastion.

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:24.960
Now, the checkbox over
here allows you to enable

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:31.240
or disable remote desktop for
this specific app service plan.

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:36.360
And to use Bastion
to securely connect

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:39.270
to the underlying instance,
you click on "Instances".

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:40.800
Now, I only have one instance,

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:43.410
so you'll see one instance
being listed here.

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:45.270
Imagine if I had
multiple instances,

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:47.220
you will see all
of them over here.

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:49.950
But any of these -- and if I
need to restart this instance,

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:55.730
you can click on "Restart" and
the instance will be restarted.

00:36:55.730 --> 00:36:58.790
To connect to a secure RDP,

00:36:58.790 --> 00:37:03.030
just click on "Connect",
and there you go.

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.840
So you can see I already
have the Reg Edit open,

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:12.150
so I can go ahead
and verify that yes,

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.520
my registry is created.

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:16.170
I can look at the event log.

00:37:16.170 --> 00:37:19.470
I can look at the MMC snap-ins,
so I can actually see

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:23.970
that the SMTP role is
successfully configured.

00:37:23.970 --> 00:37:29.010
I have access to the
underlying IIS server.

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:33.360
And for any reason, if I want
to troubleshoot further on SQL,

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:35.310
then you can see there's
a folder called that

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:38.520
"Install Scripts," and with
the name of your script,

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:42.120
we will create a log of folders

00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:45.120
where you can actually see the
log of the installation files.

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:49.080
So yeah it's -- you
can do all of that.

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:55.290
And if you want to see the
overall adaptive log on the C:,

00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:58.410
you can actually find
this adapter.log file.

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:02.880
Now, one word of advice over
here that while you can do RDP,

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:05.130
but remember, if you
make any change,

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:08.850
any configuration change, or
any installation while you are

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:12.830
in the RDP mode, those
changes are not dominant,

00:38:12.830 --> 00:38:17.460
meaning for any reason if this
instance is restarted again,

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:18.840
all your changes will be lost.

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.870
So the right way is that
you please include all your

00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:26.430
installers or configurations as
part of installation script,

00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:30.660
put it in a zip file along
with any required MSIs,

00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:33.720
and then upload it, follow the
steps that I already demoed,

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:38.460
so that across the scale out,
scaleup, and restarts all

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:43.390
of those changes and
configurations are persisted.

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.140
I'm going to close this.

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:52.650
So that's how the
install zip looks like,

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:57.390
it's just a storage account,
