00:38:57.390 --> 00:39:02.250
Now, as you can see here,
that I have this app,

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:05.790
which this is the app that
we created within the plan.

00:39:05.790 --> 00:39:08.970
Now, again, there is no
difference between an app

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:12.390
on a Managed Instance
plan when compared

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:15.780
to a standard app
server Windows plan.

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:17.490
So that is the power.

00:39:17.490 --> 00:39:19.620
That's the beauty
of the solution.

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:23.040
So now if I really need
to scale out or to scale

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:27.540
up the experience as
consistent, it remains same.

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:29.490
I can now use deployment slots,

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:32.670
I can configure
deployment center.

00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:36.810
So imagine you have this
application on-prem,

00:39:36.810 --> 00:39:39.660
and now you have migrated
your application

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:41.640
without making pretty
much any change

00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:44.550
or maybe just minor
changes to your code,

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:47.970
you are now on Azure App
Service, you can use the power

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:50.580
of quick deployments
using slots,

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:54.810
you can make quick changes
to environment variables,

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:57.480
you can configure
authentication, use identity,

00:39:57.480 --> 00:40:03.000
or set up resiliency using
backups, you will get support

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:07.500
from custom domain certificates,
and you can even scale up

00:40:07.500 --> 00:40:10.950
and you can scale
out based on rules

00:40:10.950 --> 00:40:12.690
or you can do that manually.

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:14.670
So that -- these are
some of the benefits.

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:19.120
Now, I'm going to take
